Give gen_init_cpio the two Linux interfaces Darwin does not have.

The program that packs a built-in initramfs opens its output with O_LARGEFILE
and copies file contents with copy_file_range. Neither exists on Darwin: the
flag is how a 32-bit Linux asks for 64-bit offsets, which Darwin gives
unconditionally, and the syscall is a kernel-side copy this file already has a
read/write fallback for two lines below the call.

So the flag becomes zero and the syscall becomes a shim that fails with ENOSYS,
which is the path the fallback was written for. Both are inside #ifdef __APPLE__.

--- a/usr/gen_init_cpio.c	2026-09-06 14:57:24
+++ b/usr/gen_init_cpio.c	2026-09-06 14:57:25
@@ -14,7 +14,30 @@
 #include <ctype.h>
 #include <limits.h>
 
+#ifdef __APPLE__
 /*
+ * Two Linux-isms this program reaches for, on a host that has neither.
+ *
+ * O_LARGEFILE says "64-bit offsets please" to a 32-bit Linux; Darwin's are
+ * always 64-bit, so the flag has nothing to ask for and zero is what it means
+ * here. copy_file_range is a kernel-side copy with a read/write fallback right
+ * behind it in this file -- the shim reports the failure that fallback already
+ * handles, rather than reimplementing the copy a second time.
+ */
+#define O_LARGEFILE 0
+
+static inline ssize_t copy_file_range(int fd_in, off_t *off_in, int fd_out,
+				      off_t *off_out, size_t len,
+				      unsigned int flags)
+{
+	(void)fd_in; (void)off_in; (void)fd_out;
+	(void)off_out; (void)len; (void)flags;
+	errno = ENOSYS;
+	return -1;
+}
+#endif
+
+/*
